<header class="entry-header">
<h1 class="entry-title">A Polite Lie Is Still A Dangerous One</h1>			</header>
<div class="entry-content">
<p><br>While &#8220;What Up, I&#8217;m Three&#8221; wasn&#8217;t my favorite bit from A Black Lady Sketch Show, it&#8217;s still pretty funny, and it&#8217;s extremely relevant right now.  (NSFW clip due to swearing.)</p>
<figure class="wp-block-embed is-type-video is-provider-youtube wp-block-embed-youtube wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio"><div class="wp-block-embed__wrapper"> <iframe title="A Black Lady Sketch Show | What Up I&#039;m Three (Full Sketch) | HBO" width="790" height="444" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen data-src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/APP_ZsTniCs?feature=oembed" class="lazyload"></iframe></div></figure>
<p>The premise is pretty simple: A woman has adopted a &#8220;three year old&#8221; who is clearly a full-grown adult… but the adult absolutely <em>insists</em> that she is three years old. Her friend won&#8217;t have any of it, because she <em>knows</em> the &#8220;three-year-old&#8221; is running some kind of scam … but pointing it out only makes her friend angrier and defend the &#8220;three-year-old&#8221; even more.</p>
<figure class="wp-block-image alignleft size-full"><img wpfc-lazyload-disable="true" decoding="async" width="320" height="180" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAUAAAAC0AQAAAADNn8i8AAAAAnRSTlMAAHaTzTgAAAAeSURBVFjD7cExAQAAAMKg9U9tDQ+gAAAAAAAAAH4NHNQAARczmc0AAAAASUVORK5CYII=" alt="" class="wp-image-144705 lazyload" data-src="https://ideatrash.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Duz8Gr.gif" data-eio-rwidth="320" data-eio-rheight="180"/><noscript><img wpfc-lazyload-disable="true" decoding="async" width="320" height="180" src="https://ideatrash.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Duz8Gr.gif" alt="" class="wp-image-144705" data-eio="l"/></noscript></figure>
<p>Of <em>course</em> the &#8220;three-year-old&#8221; is up to no good.</p>
<p>Calling yourself — or an organization — by a name that hides or misrepresents the group&#8217;s focus is a clear sign that they&#8217;re hiding something, and that the thing they&#8217;re hiding is not good for <em>you.</em></p>
<p>For example, Consumer Energy Alliance is &#8220;a front group for utilities and the fossil fuel industry, helped to pioneer deceptive tactics that other utilities and fossil fuel interests would later employ in their efforts to slow solar power’s growth. Calling itself the &#8216;voice of the energy consumer,&#8217; <a href="https://lastchancealliance.org/a-decade-of-deception-exposed-new-report-on-big-oils-front-groups-in-california" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">CEA’s members include ExxonMobil, Chevron and Shell Oil.</a>&#8221; Color me suspicious, but when I think &#8220;energy consumer,&#8221; I do <em>not</em> think of the people who own oil refineries. Consumers are the <em>customers</em> of the energy producers.</p>
<div style="clear:both; margin-top:1em; margin-bottom:1em;"><a href="https://ideatrash.net/2024/03/in-america-religious-freedom-is-a-theocratic-dogwhistle.html" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" class="scdbd2af3f344a3407851d0fc703a402b"><div style="padding-left:1em; padding-right:1em;"><span class="ctaText">See also</span>&nbsp; <span class="postTitle">In America, "Religious Freedom" Is A Theocratic Dogwhistle</span></div></a></div><p>It&#8217;s not just energy or conservation issues either; it&#8217;s in every sector. For example, &#8220;<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Association_of_American_Physicians_and_Surgeons" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">The Association of American Physicians and Surgeons</a>&#8221; sounds like it&#8217;s a mainstream, respectable group. The name of the group is even sounds a lot like that of other prestigious medical groups. But The Association of American Physicians and Surgeons is anything <em>but</em> reputable. It&#8217;s a politically conservative non-profit that &#8220;promotes conspiracy theories and medical misinformation, such as HIV/AIDS denialism, climate change denial, the abortion–breast cancer hypothesis, and vaccine and autism connections.&#8221; Or <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/America%27s_Frontline_Doctors" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">&#8220;America&#8217;s Frontline Doctors,&#8221;</a> which is another political organization of COVID-19 deniers, anti-maskers, and anti-vaccination activists.</p>
<p>But people have started to get wise to that trick&#8230; so the propaganda machine has gone a step further, and is hiding even <em>those</em> misleading names behind generalities and euphemisms.</p>
<p>For example, this Fox News article, &#8220;Doctors on key US health task force accused of prioritizing DEI over evidence-based medicine.&#8221; (<a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20251027021822/https://www.foxnews.com/politics/doctors-key-us-health-task-force-accused-prioritizing-dei-over-evidence-based-medicine" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Internet Archive</a>, <a href="https://archive.is/AYdpD" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Archive.Is</a>, <a href="https://ghostarchive.org/archive/1x5Ak" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">GhostArchive</a>.) </p>
<p>Typically, you would expect to see who is accused of what.  And the Fox story definitely tells you what the accusations are, and who is being accused.</p>
<p>But you would <em>also</em> expect to hear <strong>who was doing the accusing</strong>.  </p>
<p>Instead, there are four full paragraphs (several hundred words, or several screens down) before the &#8220;news&#8221; story finally gets around to mentioning who the accusers are. Even then, it&#8217;s only <em>implied</em>:</p>
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p>&#8220;HHS has been made aware of the ideological issues with members of the USPSTF raised by letters from Senate Republicans, members of the GOP Doctors Caucus, and a large group of physicians including Associations of American Physicians and Surgeons, America’s Frontline Doctors, and the Pennsylvania Direct Primary Care Association. HHS is troubled by these allegations and is investigating further,&#8221; Emily Hilliard, a Health and Human Services Department spokesperson told Fox News Digital when asked about Kennedy&#8217;s plans for the future of the current USPSTF.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But even <em>that</em> sentence — again, hidden halfway down the story — is misleading.  That implies that all of those groups &#8212; a mix of Senators, and activist groups of varying and questionable reputation &#8212; had the same complaints, that they were working together, and shared the same validity and expertise. </p>
<div style="clear:both; margin-top:1em; margin-bottom:1em;"><a href="https://ideatrash.net/2023/07/i-believe-there-is-a-freudian-slip-in-your-meme.html" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" class="scdbd2af3f344a3407851d0fc703a402b"><div style="padding-left:1em; padding-right:1em;"><span class="ctaText">See also</span>&nbsp; <span class="postTitle">I believe there is a Freudian slip in your meme.</span></div></a></div><p><strong>While I have huge problems with the goals of these particular groups, that is not the point.</strong></p>
<p><strong>My problem is with the reporting.</strong></p>
<p>These groups are putting a lot of effort into implying that they are something they are not. They are clearly trying to lie to the American people.</p>
<p>And so in order to continue with their big lie, even the existence of these radical political groups is obscured behind passive sentences and generalized euphemisms.</p>
<p>That is not a mistake.</p>
<p>That is a <em>choice</em>.</p>
<p>Every time one of these organizations — or the &#8220;journalists&#8221; who report on them — does this, they are straight-up looking you in your eyesockets, saying, &#8220;What up, they&#8217;re three.&#8221;</p>
<p>They think you&#8217;re going to just go along with it.</p>
<p>They expect you to just go along with it.</p>
<p>Are they right?</p>
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>
<p>Featured Image by <a href="https://pixabay.com/users/readyelements-8569947/?utm_source=link-attribution&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_campaign=image&amp;utm_content=3509489" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Mahesh Patel</a> from <a href="https://pixabay.com//?utm_source=link-attribution&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_campaign=image&amp;utm_content=3509489" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Pixabay</a></p></div><footer class="entry-footer">
